diagnosis_text:
After exhaustive inspection of every network and application layer, no fault was found anywhere in the ospf_enterprise_static topology. The user's report that web2.local and web3.local are unreachable from host_1_1_1_1 is not confirmed by any live diagnostic evidence.

Specifically: ICMP from host_1_1_1_1 reaches both web_server_2 (10.200.0.5) and web_server_3 (10.200.0.6) with zero packet loss. Direct HTTP requests (curl) via the hostnames web2.local and web3.local both return HTTP 200 OK with correct page content immediately. DNS resolution works correctly — the dns_server (10.200.0.2) has accurate A records in /etc/bind/db.local mapping web2 → 10.200.0.5 and web3 → 10.200.0.6, and nslookup confirms those records are served as expected. Apache is running on both servers with a valid index.html, and no iptables rules exist on any server or switch to block traffic. The OSPF routing plane is fully converged across all six FRR devices, with complete routing tables and correctly configured areas. The switch_server_access bridge includes all server-facing ports (eth0–eth4) in br0 and ARP entries for all web servers are correctly populated.

The same positive reachability was confirmed from host_2_1_1_1 as well, confirming there is no path-specific blackhole. The ticket describes a symptom that does not currently exist in the network; this is a false-premise report. No corrective action is required from the network side — the issue is likely a stale browser cache or a transient client-side problem that has already self-resolved.
=== END ===